  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nHigh pressure sliding in from the Great Lakes will allow for a \nwonderful Saturday across the region. Northerly flow will keep \ntemperatures pleasant today, topping out in the low to mid 60s \nwhich is about average for this time of year. A few fair weather\ncumulus clouds are expected across portions of the area this \nafternoon, dissipating by sundown. With high pressure and light \nwinds tonight, temps will fall into the upper 30s to low 40s \nacross the region. The high pressure system will slide east of \nthe area as the night progresses, bringing southerly flow back \nto the area by the early morning hours. \n\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \n\nSunday will see chances for rain from 2 different systems riding\none broad longwave trough sitting over the region. The first \nsystem will be a low moving up the eastern seaboard, generating\nscattered light rain showers across NEPA into the Catskills. \nThese showers are forecast to start by mid-morning and last into\nthe late afternoon hours. The second system will be a low \ntracking across Ontario that will push a pre-frontal trough into\nthe northern portion of the CWA that could kick off some \nscattered rain showers cold front into the area Sunday evening. \nThe main chance for rain showers however will be from a this low\npressure system dragging a cold front through the region late \nSunday night into Monday morning. Best chance for rain will be \nalong and north of the Mohawk Valley, but isolated showers may \ndevelop through the Finger Lakes into portions of the Southern \nTier. CAA behind the front will allow for some lake effect rain\nshowers to develop into portions of S Oneida county into \nMadison and E Onondaga. These showers could last into the early \nafternoon before another high pressure system moving in from \nthe west cuts them off. \n\nTemperatures during next week are going to start off cool as the\nhigh pressure system funnels cool and dry Canadian air into the\nregion. Temps on Monday will climb into the upper 50s to low \n60s Monday with highs on Tuesday only reaching the low to mid\n50s for most with some upper 50s in the warmer valleys of NEPA.\nLake effect clouds will also help keep afternoon temps cool as\nwe expect them to develop on both Monday and Tuesday thanks to\ncold air at 850mb running over the warm waters of Lake America \nand the Finger Lakes. Lows on Monday night and Tuesday night\nwill be some of the coldest of the season with widespread low to\nmid 30s expected. Even though portions of CNY have ended the\ngrowing season, frost was introduced into the forecast across\nmuch of the area as these two nights will could see the first \nwidespread frosts of the fall season. A few of the colder spots \ncould fall below freezing, especially across northern Oneida \ncounty where upper 20s are forecast. The ridge axis and \nassociated surface high will slide east of the area by Wednesday\nmorning, allowing for southerly flow to return and bring a \nwarmer, more moist air back into the region. Temps look to be \nnear normal through the rest of the week, with highs in the 60s \nand lows in the 40s.",
